\details{
A location \code{tau = k} means that the change starts at observation \code{k + 1};
\code{tau = 0} means no change. With \code{pruning = "bagel"} or \code{"LP-M"}, the algorithm uses the merging rules to combine neighbouring candidates into groups.
\code{taus()} gives each group's right endpoint, and \code{ratios()} gives the relative weights of
the locations within it. \code{weights()} combines the group weight with
these ratios to recover a weight for each location.

With \code{pruning = "LP-P"}, candidates are removed instead of merged.
Removed locations are absent from \code{weights()}, so use \code{taus()} to find
which candidates remain. Weights are not renormalised immediately after
removal and may therefore sum to less than one.

For a sequential detector, \code{weights_tau_zero()} is the current scalar
no-change weight. For a fitted result it gives the history of these weights,
one for each processed observation. This history is shorter than the input
if the analysis stopped early. The first no-change weight is \code{p0}.
}
\section{Coefficient and variance parameters}{

The \code{sigma} matrices returned by \code{\link[=active_posteriors]{active_posteriors()}} are covariance
factors. Multiplying by observation variance gives conditional coefficient
covariance. For known variance, the multiplier is the constructor's
\code{sigma^2}. Unknown-variance models use inverse-gamma shape \code{nu} and scale
\code{iota}, with density proportional to
\eqn{v^{-(\nu + 1)} \exp(-\iota/v)} at variance \eqn{v}.

For sequential detectors, \code{mus()} and \code{sigmas()} also return lists of
mean column matrices and covariance factors, in active-particle order.
\code{nu()} and \code{iota()} each return a numeric scalar for the no-change particle
only. We use \code{\link[=active_posteriors]{active_posteriors()}} when we need every particle's parameters
or are inspecting a fitted result.
}

